893 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "850858f3-9f08-5b6d-b191-4d25dc883811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569935 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dd980177-3d4f-5c6d-a6cb-2c5b9aaa3857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569937 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569937 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569937 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569937 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e295c467-cba5-5269-8303-2482a80e3bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569947 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569947 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569947 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569947 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569947 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eff3eb79-2efb-59dd-8b16-b566385dd9e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the expected value of one roll of a four-sided die?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0298a94b-23a5-5b8c-89b1-b11dd1bda638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569954 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the roll of a four-sided die. Then the support is $S=\\{1,2,3,4\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=f(3)=f(4)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)++3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1071 "One way to bet in roulette is to place $\\$1$ on a single number. If the ball lands on that number, the player wins $\\$35.$ If the ball lands on any of the other $37$ slots, the player doesn't win. What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays one hundred games on number $12$ in a row?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "068e9bc7-6f46-5ac9-a057-d5f4aa9a8aeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569958 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after one hundred games, we need to subtract $100\\cdot 1=100$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{38}$ | $\\frac{37}{38}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $35$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =35\\cdot f(35)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =35(\\frac{1}{38})+0(\\frac{37}{38}) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{38} \\\\  & ≈0.92.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $100$ games  on number $12$ is\n\n$$\n100\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=100\\cdot (0.92)=92.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $100$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=92-100=-8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1071 "One way to bet in roulette is to place $\\$2$ on a single number. If the ball lands on that number, the player wins $\\$70.$ If the ball lands on any of the other $37$ slots, the player doesn't win. What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays one hundred games on number $8$ in a row?\n\nThe expected profit in dollars is $E(p)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "267e0e90-b604-5e86-ad43-795951237ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569970 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after one hundred games, we need to subtract $100\\cdot 2=200$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{38}$ | $\\frac{37}{38}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $70$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =70\\cdot f(70)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =70(\\frac{1}{38})+0(\\frac{37}{38}) \\\\  & =\\frac{70}{38} \\\\  & ≈1.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $100$ games on number $8$ is\n\n$$\n100\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=100\\cdot (1.84)=184.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $200$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=184-200=-16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1071 "A quality control manager at a warehouse inspects electronic boxes for defective items. The number of faulty items in each box was recorded and summarized below. If $X$ is the number of faulty items in a box, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Boxes | $13$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Faulty Items | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41c4d9f2-fb24-5742-893b-e72bc5478b30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569983 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the faulty items, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of boxes is $13+6+4+2=25,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{13}{25} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{25} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{2}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{13}{25}$ | $\\frac{6}{25}$ | $\\frac{4}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{13}{25})+1(\\frac{6}{25})+2(\\frac{4}{25})+3(\\frac{2}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}+\\frac{8}{25}+\\frac{6}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+8+6}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1071 "A stack contains four cards, where each card is labeled $1$, $4$, $6$, or $9.$ If one card is randomly drawn, what is its expected value?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "522d89ff-41d6-50ec-8d1b-a4ada325fbc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569987 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the label on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S=\\{1,4,6,9\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(4)=f(6)=f(9)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+9\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{4})+6(\\frac{1}{4})+9(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+4+6+9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1071 "The rolls of an unfair octahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53e1317a-3ae2-58ac-bb4d-59a740997145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569988 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+2(\\frac{1}{16})+3(\\frac{1}{16})+4(\\frac{3}{32})+5(\\frac{1}{8})+6(\\frac{1}{32})+7(\\frac{1}{4})+8(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{12}{32}+\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{32}+\\frac{4}{32}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{12}{32}+\\frac{20}{32}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{56}{32}+\\frac{64}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4+6+12+20+6+56+64}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{172}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1071 "The number of heads facing upward after tossing $3$ unfair coins follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the number of heads facing upward after tossing the $3$ coins? Do not round the answer.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f220269-fce2-5308-a544-a882143be2b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569999 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{2}{9})+1(\\frac{1}{3})+2(\\frac{1}{9})+3(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3+2+9}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1071 "Several children were asked how many siblings they have. The number of siblings of each child was recorded below. If $X$ is the number of siblings of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of siblings | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $1$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "700fffbb-cd63-5378-9077-32b4c2add6e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570000 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of siblings, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $1+5+3+1=10,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{10})+1(\\frac{1}{2})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+5+6+3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{10} \\\\  & =1.4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair spinner has six sides labeled with the numbers $-2,$ $-1,$ $0,$ $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$ If the spinner is spun, what is the expected value of the outcome?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9cdf7c4f-4409-57f0-b1eb-dca5de834f65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570012 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the outcome of the spinner. Then the support is $S=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{6}:$\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(-1)=f(0)=f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-2\\cdot f(-2)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1)+0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{1}{6})+(-1)(\\frac{1}{6})+0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{1}{6}+0+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1+0+1+2+3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1071 "A stack of cards has $2$ cards labeled with four and $6$ cards labeled with ten. If one card is randomly drawn from the stack, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceddea1b-a5cb-5c22-91ab-6571fb7ef388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570023 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S=\\{4,10\\}.$\n\nIn the stack there are $2$ cards labeled with four and $6$ cards labeled with ten (eight cards in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(10) & =\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =4\\cdot f(4)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4})+10(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1071 "Several households were asked the number of children that lived there. The number of children in each was recorded below. If $X$ is the number of children that live at a randomly selected household, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Children | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Households | $6$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d599b4ec-9437-5cdd-aa0b-91b4481a6586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570029 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of childen, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of households is $3+9+6+6=24,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{24}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{6}{24}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{3}{24}=\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{3}{8})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3+3+2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the expected value of one roll of a seven-sided die?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d66ccea1-6118-5ac8-bad9-91d326fbbfdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570030 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the roll of a standard six-sided die. Then the support is $S=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=…=f(7)=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+…+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+…+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+…+\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6+7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{7} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1071 "The sum of the outcomes of one roll of two unfair four-sided dice follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the sum of the outcomes for one roll of the two dice?\n\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{40}$ | $\\frac{7}{40}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc6b2040-54a4-56f7-9767-230b7779b10f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570032 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & \\,\\,+6\\cdot f(6)+7\\cdot f(7)+8\\cdot f(8) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{40})+3(\\frac{7}{40})+4(\\frac{1}{5})+5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+6(\\frac{3}{20})+7(\\frac{1}{10})+8(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{40}+\\frac{21}{40}+\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{18}{20}+\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{40}+\\frac{21}{40}+\\frac{32}{40}+\\frac{50}{40}+\\frac{36}{40}+\\frac{28}{40}+\\frac{16}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+32+50+36+28+16}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{189}{40} \\\\  & =4.725.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
